        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains December 3 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, like 8 of 10.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For December 3 in music history, a different record was number one in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>That is the Hot 100 chart week that includes December 3.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"You Light Up My Life" had the most weeks, with ten.</li>
          <li>"Tonight's the Night (Gonna Be Alright)" came next with eight.</li>
          <li>"I Think I Love You" and "Fly, Robin, Fly" had three each.</li>
          <li>"Papa Was a Rollin' Stone" held number one for a single week.</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in their first week on top.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} were in the middle of a longer run, and {{N_LAST}} were in their last week.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>Try the <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> to see which song was on top the week you were born.</p>
        <p>It works for any 1970s birthday, not only December 3 in music history.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the December 3 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called December 3.</p>
        <p>This December 3 in music history page uses the weekly Hot 100 week that contains December 3 each year.</p>
        <p>In 1977 that week began on December 3 itself, and in the other nine years it began one to six days before:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>
        <p>Nine of the ten runs were unbroken.</p>
        <p>"You Don't Bring Me Flowers" was split by Chic's "Le Freak," so its week of run counts only its own weeks at number one.</p>
        <p>For "Tonight's the Night (Gonna Be Alright)" the weekly file has eight chart weeks at number one, which matches Wikipedia's eight consecutive weeks.</p>

        <h3>Six Songs Also on the November 28 List</h3>
        <p>This is the real week of December 3, so every song on top that week is here.</p>
        <p>Six of them are also on the <a href="/blog/trivia/november-28-in-music-history/">November 28 in music history</a> list, five days earlier.</p>
        <p>Only 1972, 1973, 1975 and 1978 had a new number one by December 3.</p>
        <p>Four songs also turn up on the <a href="/blog/trivia/december-12-in-music-history/">December 12 in music history</a> list: "Top of the World," "Fly, Robin, Fly," "Tonight's the Night (Gonna Be Alright)" and "You Light Up My Life."</p>

        <h2 id="early">December 3 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: A TV Family, Shaft and a Seven-Minute Single</h2>
        <p>December 3 in music history opened the decade with a song from a TV show.</p>
        <p>Isaac Hayes was in his last week on top in 1971.</p>
        <p>The Temptations got a single week of their own in 1972.</p>

        <h3>1970: "I Think I Love You" by The Partridge Family</h3>
        <p>"I Think I Love You" was recorded in 1970 at United Western Recorders in Hollywood, with Wes Farrell producing.</p>
        <p>Billy Strange led the session and wrote the arrangement, and Hal Blaine played drums.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the Bahler brothers, Ron Hicklin and Jackie Ward sang backing vocals, as they did on every later Partridge Family record.</p>
        <p>The single also reached number one in Canada in November 1970 and in Australia in 1971.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows three weeks at number one, from November 21 to December 5, 1970.</p>
        <p>The Jackson 5's "I'll Be There" was on top before it, and Smokey Robinson &amp; the Miracles' "The Tears of a Clown" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains December 3 was the second of three.</p>
        <p>More bright radio hits of the decade are on the <a href="/blog/genres/pop-songs-of-70s/">pop songs of the 70s</a> list.</p>

        <h3>1971: "Theme from Shaft" by Isaac Hayes</h3>
        <p>Isaac Hayes recorded the rhythm parts of "Theme from Shaft" first, then scored the rest of the film before coming back to the theme.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Hayes played the synthesized keyboard himself.</p>
        <p>It reached number 4 on the UK Singles Chart.</p>
        <p>At the 1972 Grammy Awards, Hayes won Best Instrumental Arrangement for the theme and Best Original Score for the film.</p>
        <p>The 1971 recording entered the Grammy Hall of Fame in 1999.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, from November 20 to November 27, 1971.</p>
        <p>Cher's "Gypsys, Tramps &amp; Thieves" was on top before it, and Sly and the Family Stone's "Family Affair" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains December 3 was the second and last of two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 89 on the 1971 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>It fits with the records on the <a href="/blog/genres/70s-funk/">70s funk</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1972: "Papa Was a Rollin' Stone" by The Temptations</h3>
        <p>Norman Whitfield and Barrett Strong wrote "Papa Was a Rollin' Stone" in 1971.</p>
        <p>The Undisputed Truth recorded it first, and Whitfield produced their version, released as a single in May 1972.</p>
        <p>The Temptations' cover came out as a single in September 1972, edited down to about seven minutes.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says their version won three Grammy Awards in 1973, and it entered the Grammy Hall of Fame in 1999.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of December 2, 1972.</p>
        <p>Johnny Nash's "I Can See Clearly Now" was on top before it, and Helen Reddy's <a href="/blog/songs/i-am-woman/">"I Am Woman"</a> came next.</p>
        <p>The song is number 100 on the 1973 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of the Motown sound is on the <a href="/blog/genres/70s-soul-music/">70s soul music</a> page.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">December 3 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: The Carpenters, a Borrowed Organ and a Disco Instrumental</h2>
        <p>December 3 in music history in the middle of the decade has two new arrivals and one farewell.</p>
        <p>The Carpenters and Silver Convention both reached number one that week.</p>
        <p>Billy Swan was in his second and last week on top in 1974.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Top of the World" by Carpenters</h3>
        <p>Richard Carpenter wrote the music for "Top of the World," and John Bettis wrote the lyrics.</p>
        <p>Lynn Anderson recorded it for an album also called Top of the World, and her version reached number 2 on the US country chart.</p>
        <p>The Carpenters' own version came out as a single on September 17, 1973.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it topped the US pop chart for two weeks in December 1973.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, from December 1 to December 8, 1973.</p>
        <p>Ringo Starr's "Photograph" was on top before it, and Charlie Rich's "The Most Beautiful Girl" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains December 3 was the first of two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 39 on the 1974 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of the duo's hits are on the <a href="/blog/artists/carpenters-songs/">Carpenters songs</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1974: "I Can Help" by Billy Swan</h3>
        <p>Billy Swan recorded "I Can Help" in March 1974 at Young 'Un Sound in Murfreesboro, with Chip Young producing and engineering.</p>
        <p>While writing it, Swan used a "Rock" preset on his Rhythm Master drum machine.</p>
        <p>He played the keyboard part on a portable Farfisa organ that belonged to Memphis session musician Bobby Emmons.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Young's German Shepherd puppy, Bowser, tugged on Swan's pants leg during the take.</p>
        <p>Chip Young added the electric guitar solo after the main recording.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, from November 23 to November 30, 1974.</p>
        <p>John Lennon's "Whatever Gets You thru the Night" was on top before it, and Carl Douglas's "Kung Fu Fighting" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains December 3 was the second and last of two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 44 on the 1975 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>It sits well with the crossover hits on the <a href="/blog/genres/70s-country-songs/">70s country songs</a> list.</p>

        <h3>1975: "Fly, Robin, Fly" by Silver Convention</h3>
        <p>Sylvester Levay and Stephan Prager wrote "Fly, Robin, Fly," and Prager produced it.</p>
        <p>It won the Grammy Award for Best R&amp;B Instrumental Performance.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows three weeks at number one, from November 29 to December 13, 1975.</p>
        <p>KC and the Sunshine Band's <a href="/blog/songs/thats-the-way-i-like-it/">"That's the Way (I Like It)"</a> was on top before it and again after it.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains December 3 was the first of three.</p>
        <p>The song is number 14 on the 1976 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>Hear more of the sound on the <a href="/blog/genres/disco-music-of-the-70s/">disco music of the 70s</a> page.</p>

        <h2 id="late">December 3 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: Rod Stewart, Debby Boone and Two Streisand Duets</h2>
        <p>December 3 in music history closed the decade with two long runs and two Barbra Streisand duets.</p>
        <p>Rod Stewart was three weeks into eight, and Debby Boone was in her eighth week of ten.</p>
        <p>Streisand sang with Neil Diamond in 1978 and with Donna Summer in 1979.</p>

        <h3>1976: "Tonight's the Night (Gonna Be Alright)" by Rod Stewart</h3>
        <p>Tom Dowd produced "Tonight's the Night (Gonna Be Alright)" for Rod Stewart's 1976 album A Night on the Town.</p>
        <p>It was recorded at Muscle Shoals Sound Studio in Sheffield, Alabama, and the final vocal was cut at Caribou Ranch.</p>
        <p>The song entered the Hot 100 at number 81 on October 2, 1976.</p>
        <p>It peaked at number 5 in the UK.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows eight weeks at number one, from November 13, 1976, to January 1, 1977.</p>
        <p>Steve Miller Band's "Rock'n Me" was on top before it, and Marilyn McCoo and Billy Davis Jr.'s "You Don't Have to Be a Star (To Be in My Show)" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains December 3 was the third of eight.</p>
        <p>The song is number 1 on the 1977 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>He is one of the voices on the <a href="/blog/artists/70s-singers/">70s singers</a> list.</p>

        <h3>1977: "You Light Up My Life" by Debby Boone</h3>
        <p>Joseph Brooks wrote "You Light Up My Life," and Kasey Cisyk recorded it first, for the soundtrack album of the 1977 film of the same title.</p>
        <p>That soundtrack album peaked at number 17 on the Billboard 200.</p>
        <p>Debby Boone's single was certified platinum by the RIAA.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says her hit led to the 1978 Grammy Award for Best New Artist.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows ten weeks at number one, from October 15 to December 17, 1977.</p>
        <p>Meco's "Star Wars Theme/Cantina Band" was on top before it, and the Bee Gees' <a href="/blog/songs/how-deep-is-your-love/">"How Deep Is Your Love"</a> came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains December 3 was the eighth of ten.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>Find more ballads like it on the <a href="/blog/songs/70s-love-songs/">70s love songs</a> list.</p>

        <h3>1978: "You Don't Bring Me Flowers" by Barbra Streisand and Neil Diamond</h3>
        <p>Neil Diamond wrote "You Don't Bring Me Flowers" with Alan and Marilyn Bergman.</p>
        <p>It was meant to be the theme for Norman Lear's sitcom All That Glitters, but Lear changed the show's concept, so Diamond turned it into a full song.</p>
        <p>Diamond released a solo version on his 1977 album I'm Glad You're Here with Me Tonight, and Streisand covered it on her 1978 album Songbird.</p>
        <p>Louisville radio program director Gary Guthrie spliced the two solo tracks into a duet.</p>
        <p>Columbia then had the pair record a new studio version together, released on October 17, 1978.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, in two separate stretches.</p>
        <p>Donna Summer's <a href="/blog/songs/macarthur-park-donna-summer/">"MacArthur Park"</a> was on top before it, and the duet's first week was the chart week of December 2, 1978.</p>
        <p>Chic's "Le Freak" took over the next week, the duet returned for the week of December 16, and then "Le Freak" came back, so the December 3 week was the first of the duet's two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 21 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More quiet records of the decade are on the <a href="/blog/songs/70s-slow-songs/">slow songs of the 70s</a> list.</p>

        <h3>1979: "No More Tears (Enough Is Enough)" by Barbra Streisand and Donna Summer</h3>
        <p>"No More Tears (Enough Is Enough)" was recorded in Los Angeles in August 1979 at the Village Recorder.</p>
        <p>Gary Klein and Giorgio Moroder produced it, and Harold Faltermeyer added production to Summer's 12-inch version.</p>
        <p>The full song is on Streisand's album Wet, and an extended version is on Summer's On the Radio: Greatest Hits Volumes I &amp; II.</p>
        <p>That 12-inch version runs 11 minutes 43 seconds, while the Wet album version runs 8:19.</p>
        <p>The 7-inch single was certified platinum.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, from November 24 to December 1, 1979.</p>
        <p>The Commodores' "Still" was on top before it, and Styx's "Babe" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains December 3 was the second and last of two.</p>
        <p>More of her hits are on the <a href="/blog/artists/donna-summer/">Donna Summer</a> page.</p>

