            alt="Run chart of each December 3 number one by week, with the week that contains December 3 highlighted in gold, from the single week of Papa Was a Rollin' Stone to the eighth of You Light Up My Life's ten, with a gap in You Don't Bring Me Flowers' run">
        </picture>
        <p class="post-img-caption">Each box is one week at number one and the gold box is the week that contains December 3.</p>
        <p class="post-img-caption">Chart: Music of the 70s, from this site's weekly Billboard Hot 100 data.</p>

        <h3>Where December 3 Fell in Each Run</h3>
        <p>The weeks split three ways:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} songs were in their first week at number one: "Papa Was a Rollin' Stone," "Top of the World," "Fly, Robin, Fly" and "You Don't Bring Me Flowers."</li>
          <li>"Papa Was a Rollin' Stone" lasted only that one week.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} were in the middle of a longer run: "I Think I Love You," "Tonight's the Night (Gonna Be Alright)" and "You Light Up My Life."</li>
          <li>{{N_LAST}} were in the last week of their run: "Theme from Shaft," "I Can Help" and "No More Tears (Enough Is Enough)."</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Test Your Own Memory</h3>
        <p>Could you have named all ten December 3 in music history number ones, from The Partridge Family in 1970 to Barbra Streisand and Donna Summer in 1979?</p>
        <p>Other weeks have their own lists, including <a href="/blog/trivia/november-19-in-music-history/">November 19 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/november-12-in-music-history/">November 12 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/november-7-in-music-history/">November 7 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/may-1-in-music-history/">May 1 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/october-8-in-music-history/">October 8 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/february-1-in-music-history/">February 1 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/september-16-in-music-history/">September 16 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/august-19-in-music-history/">August 19 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/july-15-in-music-history/">July 15 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/june-12-in-music-history/">June 12 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/may-29-in-music-history/">May 29 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/april-17-in-music-history/">April 17 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/march-20-in-music-history/">March 20 in music history</a>, <a href="/blog/trivia/february-13-in-music-history/">February 13 in music history</a> and <a href="/blog/trivia/january-9-in-music-history/">January 9 in music history</a>.</p>
        <p>For a quick game, the <a href="/pages/70s-trivia-quiz">70s Music Trivia Quiz</a> deals ten new questions each round.</p>
        <p>Every chart topper of the decade is on the <a href="/blog/songs/70s-number-one-hits/">70s number one hits</a> list.</p>
        <p>More of the decade lives on the <a href="/">Music of the 70s</a> home page.</p>

